J in the per-bit family. (O1) Each π_J is a fixed total Boolean function; per-bit decomposition is parameter-controlled but each π_J is not oracle-dependent. (O2) π_J answers "is bit J of π(x) set", not "is z complicated"; no self-referential Kt inequality. (O3) The per-bit family supplies J*(N) ≈ N − log₂ N strings per N, but they are FIXED, not traversal-path-dependent fresh prefixes — Brandt's Lemma 2 needs an infinite supply of growing-length Kt-random prefixes generated on demand by oracle access, not a finite stack of pre-determined strings. (O4) Uniform DTIME bound, not circuit. The "minimal weakening of O1 admitting Brandt's TRAVERSE" the C3 spec asked about does NOT exist on {π_J : J = 0..N-1}. **Closure mode E** (equivalence): per-bit decomposition is structurally orthogonal to Brandt's diagonalisation skeleton; C3 reduces to E5.8 wholesale at the structural level. **Empirical novelty** (the only new content beyond E5.8 + E1.3): bounded-Kt cut location J*(N) ≈ N − log₂ N is a quantitative sharpening of E1.3's Fourier cut, expressing the limit of what stack-VM-class programs can resolve about per-bit difficulty. Detecting the smooth/oscillatory transition at the Kt level would require a richer VM with arithmetic primitives (LOG2, LI_APPROX, DIV_LOG, GEO_SUM — the R^{-1} kernel) — proposed as successor C3.a. **Cross-domain ingredient**: Brandt 2024 ePrint 2024/687 bounded-Kt simulator (3-bit stack VM), reused from S51. **Adds new finite-N empirical fact** (not previously measured): per-bit Kt-bounded cut at J*(N) ≈ N − log₂ N, gap to E1.3's 0.5N cut grows as 0.5N − log₂ N. Cites E5.8, E1.3, E1.9, E5.3. See experiments/constructions/brandt_per_bit/. | 105 |
